test_connect_exact -- 3D connect: tolerance tests and connect / exact (Nov 2026)
=======================================================================

QUICK START

  sh run_connect_exact_tests.sh [path-to-lagrit]   22 decks, about 30 s
  open logs/summary.html

WHAT IS TESTED

  Nine point sets, each connected twice: plain connect (the tolerance
  in-sphere tests, and points added in small tets) and connect / exact
  (exact in-sphere and orientation tests, src/lg_predicates3d.c; no
  points added).

    g0    12 x 12 x 12 regular grid (exact ties)
    j12   the grid jittered by up to 1e-12
    j9    by up to 1e-9
    j6a   by up to 1e-6 (seed 2)
    j6b   by up to 1e-6 (seed 5)
    j3    by up to 1e-3
    rnd   3000 random points
    thin  3000 random points in a 100 x 1 x 1 box
    gi    a 12 x 12 x 12 lattice, spacing 10, at UTM-sized x and y
    gij   that lattice moved by multiples of 1/1024 (up to 3/1024)
    sph   the createpts rtp sphere (5 radii, 11.25 degree steps),
          duplicates removed

  The points are written with 17 digits, so the checker sees exactly
  the coordinates LaGriT read. Every mesh is checked with exact
  rational arithmetic (Python fractions) where floating point cannot
  decide:
    added     points connect added
    missing   input points in no tet (dudded)
    nonDel    interior faces that are not locally Delaunay
    inverted  tets inverted or flat, exactly
    flat_fp   tets valid exactly but of zero or negative volume in
              floating point (flat to round-off)

PASS
  connect / exact: added = missing = nonDel = inverted = flat_fp = 0
  (sph: flat_fp > 0 is expected and reported, see below).
  plain connect: LaGriT finished and inverted = 0; the other numbers
  are the baseline. On the jittered grids plain connect leaves
  hundreds to thousands of faces not Delaunay, and at 1e-9 adds 281
  points and leaves 4 input points out.

THE SPHERE
  createpts rtp points are meant to lie on common planes and spheres,
  but sin and cos round them off. The exact Delaunay tetrahedralization
  of those coordinates contains tets that are flat to round-off: valid
  in exact arithmetic, zero or negative volume when computed in double
  precision (159 here). For such input use plain connect.

FILES
  make_connect_exact.py   writes the point sets, the decks and
                          work/expect.json
  check_connect_exact.py  judges them; logs/summary.html
  run_connect_exact_tests.sh
